1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,620
There is one sentence in the NATO treaty
that every member has agreed to.

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:07,520
An armed attack
against one or more of them

3
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:10,540
shall be considered
an attack against them all.

4
00:00:11,042 --> 00:00:12,662
It is Article Five.

5
00:00:12,742 --> 00:00:17,082
In more than seventy-five years,
it has been invoked exactly once.

6
00:00:17,796 --> 00:00:20,676
Not for Poland. Not for the Baltic states.

7
00:00:20,756 --> 00:00:22,836
Not for anyone in Europe.

8
00:00:23,505 --> 00:00:28,445
On the twelfth of September, 2001,
the allies agreed to invoke it.

9
00:00:29,085 --> 00:00:31,585
A few weeks later, they confirmed it.

10
00:00:32,045 --> 00:00:35,405
The attack on the United States
was an attack on them all.

11
00:00:36,136 --> 00:00:39,296
Over the next twenty years,
soldiers from dozens of countries

12
00:00:39,526 --> 00:00:41,736
followed the US into Afghanistan.

13
00:00:42,816 --> 00:00:45,396
More than a thousand of them
never came home.

14
00:00:45,983 --> 00:00:47,423
Britain lost four hundred and fifty-seven.

15
00:00:47,503 --> 00:00:50,303
Canada lost a hundred and fifty-eight.

16
00:00:50,383 --> 00:00:54,343
Denmark, a country of six million,
lost forty-four.

17
00:00:57,475 --> 00:01:00,235
Twenty-five years later,
a large survey asked Europeans

18
00:01:00,465 --> 00:01:02,695
how they see the United States.

19
00:01:03,438 --> 00:01:05,958
Only eleven per cent called it an ally.

20
00:01:06,658 --> 00:01:10,638
The most common answer was colder.
A necessary partner.

21
00:01:11,046 --> 00:01:14,346
That is not hatred. In a way, it is worse.

22
00:01:14,946 --> 00:01:16,686
It is an ally doing the maths.

23
00:01:17,482 --> 00:01:19,022
So what happens to the most powerful

24
00:01:19,252 --> 00:01:22,182
country on Earth
when its allies stop trusting it?

25
00:01:22,842 --> 00:01:25,482
Not to them. To the United States.

26
00:01:28,022 --> 00:01:30,562
The first part of the answer
is not dramatic.

27
00:01:30,762 --> 00:01:32,822
Nobody storms out.

28
00:01:33,322 --> 00:01:36,322
What happens is quieter,
and much harder to undo.

29
00:01:37,039 --> 00:01:38,979
Trust sounds like something soft.

30
00:01:39,059 --> 00:01:41,479
Something for friends and families.

31
00:01:41,739 --> 00:01:44,359
Between countries, it is a working tool.

32
00:01:44,905 --> 00:01:48,125
Imagine two countries
building a fighter jet together.

33
00:01:48,485 --> 00:01:52,065
It will take ten years,
billions in shared factories,

34
00:01:52,145 --> 00:01:54,205
and parts that only fit each other.

35
00:01:54,431 --> 00:01:59,411
If both expect the partnership to survive
the next election, that plan makes sense.

36
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:02,800
But if one of them starts to wonder
whether the other will walk away

37
00:02:03,030 --> 00:02:06,300
the moment it is convenient,
the maths changes.

38
00:02:06,831 --> 00:02:09,031
It builds a second factory at home.

39
00:02:09,671 --> 00:02:12,171
It buys from two suppliers, not one.

40
00:02:12,691 --> 00:02:15,591
It keeps a backup plan
it hopes it will never need.

41
00:02:16,232 --> 00:02:18,152
Nobody announces anything.

42
00:02:18,692 --> 00:02:20,352
Nobody leaves an alliance.

43
00:02:21,112 --> 00:02:23,652
They just stop planning
on the assumption that you'll be there,

44
00:02:23,882 --> 00:02:26,952
and start planning
for the possibility that you won't.

45
00:02:27,404 --> 00:02:30,624
And in the last few years,
the allies of the United States

46
00:02:30,854 --> 00:02:33,764
were given a lot of reasons
to do exactly that.

47
00:02:34,369 --> 00:02:38,709
Canada, which followed the US
into Afghanistan, was hit with tariffs

48
00:02:38,929 --> 00:02:41,669
and told it should become
the fifty-first state.

49
00:02:42,316 --> 00:02:46,456
Denmark, which lost forty-four soldiers
there, was told the United States

50
00:02:46,686 --> 00:02:50,456
wanted Greenland,
and for a year, would not rule out force.

51
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:55,720
Ukraine, in the middle of a war, saw US
intelligence switched off for a week.

52
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:58,480
Today, Europe is Ukraine's

53
00:02:58,710 --> 00:03:01,840
largest source of military aid,
and Europeans now pay

54
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:04,660
for many of the US weapons
that reach Ukraine.

55
00:03:05,337 --> 00:03:08,217
And when diesel at home
passed six dollars a gallon,

56
00:03:08,377 --> 00:03:11,017
Washington eased its sanctions
so that Russian diesel

57
00:03:11,247 --> 00:03:13,257
could flow to the United States.

58
00:03:13,987 --> 00:03:14,947
Russia.

59
00:03:15,027 --> 00:03:18,707
The country that many US allies
are spending billions to deter.

60
00:03:19,461 --> 00:03:23,601
Even if all of it came, it would cover
less than two weeks of US demand.

61
00:03:23,681 --> 00:03:27,841
And the United States
exports more diesel than it imports.

62
00:03:28,669 --> 00:03:31,249
Every one of these
can be explained on its own.

63
00:03:31,709 --> 00:03:36,389
A negotiating tactic. An election.
A price at the pump.

64
00:03:36,825 --> 00:03:39,405
But allies do not plan
around explanations.

65
00:03:40,185 --> 00:03:41,665
They plan around patterns.

66
00:03:42,234 --> 00:03:44,214
And the lesson they drew was simple.

67
00:03:44,914 --> 00:03:49,034
A US promise could be taken back the next
time something else mattered more.

68
00:03:51,774 --> 00:03:54,554
So the allies started building
the backup plan.

69
00:03:55,241 --> 00:03:57,901
The European Union set up
a hundred and fifty billion euros

70
00:03:58,131 --> 00:04:00,921
in loans to buy weapons together.

71
00:04:01,881 --> 00:04:04,921
And the rules favour Europe:
at least sixty-five per cent

72
00:04:05,001 --> 00:04:09,081
of the cost of the parts has to come
from Europe or its close partners.

73
00:04:09,845 --> 00:04:15,745
Spain shelved plans for the US-made F-35
fighter and is looking at European jets.

74
00:04:16,665 --> 00:04:19,845
Canada is reviewing its own F-35 order.

75
00:04:20,461 --> 00:04:22,921
In South Korea, a poll this year found

76
00:04:23,151 --> 00:04:26,481
that nearly two thirds of people
want their own nuclear weapons.

77
00:04:27,213 --> 00:04:28,613
Read that one again.

78
00:04:29,413 --> 00:04:32,893
A country protected for seventy years
by the US nuclear umbrella

79
00:04:33,123 --> 00:04:35,893
now wonders whether it needs
a bomb of its own.

80
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:40,180
That is one of the risks when security
guarantees lose their credibility.

81
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:45,540
The question stops being who will protect
us, and becomes how we protect ourselves.

82
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:49,960
And then there is Canada.

83
00:04:50,761 --> 00:04:54,701
Canada sends about two thirds
of its exports to the United States.

84
00:04:55,541 --> 00:04:57,941
The two share the longest border on Earth.

85
00:04:58,361 --> 00:05:02,541
Yet in 2026, Canada became
the first country outside Europe

86
00:05:02,771 --> 00:05:05,461
to join the EU's joint arms-buying scheme.

87
00:05:05,873 --> 00:05:08,813
And in a poll this spring,
when Canadians were asked

88
00:05:09,043 --> 00:05:12,073
whether their country should become
a full member of the European Union,

89
00:05:12,303 --> 00:05:16,993
fifty-seven per cent said they would
support it, at least somewhat.

90
00:05:17,502 --> 00:05:19,442
That is not government policy.

91
00:05:20,182 --> 00:05:22,682
Ottawa says full membership
is not on the table.

92
00:05:22,762 --> 00:05:27,482
But ask yourself why so many Canadians
are willing to entertain the idea.

93
00:05:30,252 --> 00:05:33,112
Now here is the part
that should sting in Washington.

94
00:05:33,868 --> 00:05:38,548
For decades, the United States asked its
allies to spend more on their own defence.

95
00:05:39,508 --> 00:05:40,788
It was a fair request.

96
00:05:41,425 --> 00:05:42,805
And now they are.

97
00:05:43,485 --> 00:05:48,225
NATO allies have agreed to aim for five
per cent of their economies by 2035.

98
00:05:49,203 --> 00:05:51,963
The US got exactly what it asked for.

99
00:05:52,543 --> 00:05:55,883
Except that some of that money
now builds Europe's own industry,

100
00:05:56,183 --> 00:05:59,603
so that, over time, Europe depends less
on US suppliers.

101
00:06:00,501 --> 00:06:04,181
There is a difference between asking
an ally to carry more of the load,

102
00:06:04,381 --> 00:06:07,861
and convincing it that one day
it may have to carry all of it.

103
00:06:08,426 --> 00:06:10,586
The first makes an alliance stronger.

104
00:06:11,226 --> 00:06:12,806
The second makes it optional.

105
00:06:13,223 --> 00:06:16,703
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

106
00:06:17,323 --> 00:06:18,943
It really helps the channel.

107
00:06:20,513 --> 00:06:22,733
So that is what happens to the allies.

108
00:06:23,373 --> 00:06:24,573
They hedge.

109
00:06:24,653 --> 00:06:26,853
But the question was never about them.

110
00:06:27,193 --> 00:06:29,213
It was about the United States.

111
00:06:30,001 --> 00:06:33,641
To answer that, start with a question
that sounds almost rude.

112
00:06:34,401 --> 00:06:37,141
What was the United States
actually paying for?

113
00:06:37,737 --> 00:06:41,737
After the Second World War,
it helped rebuild Western Europe,

114
00:06:41,817 --> 00:06:44,777
kept troops across the world,
and promised to defend countries

115
00:06:45,007 --> 00:06:46,637
that could not defend themselves.

116
00:06:47,282 --> 00:06:48,802
That was never charity.

117
00:06:49,542 --> 00:06:52,482
Washington had its own interests,
and it never hid them.

118
00:06:52,562 --> 00:06:55,802
But what it got back
rarely showed up on a bill.

119
00:06:56,470 --> 00:06:59,690
Take the wounded and the sick
from Iraq and Afghanistan.

120
00:07:00,410 --> 00:07:02,090
For two decades, tens of thousands

121
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:05,530
of US and allied troops
passed through one hospital.

122
00:07:06,330 --> 00:07:08,330
It was not in the United States.

123
00:07:09,010 --> 00:07:10,950
It was in Landstuhl, Germany.

124
00:07:11,569 --> 00:07:14,329
The aircraft that flew them there
landed at Ramstein,

125
00:07:14,689 --> 00:07:19,109
a US air base on German soil,
under agreements already in place.

126
00:07:19,772 --> 00:07:21,472
Or take computer chips.

127
00:07:22,172 --> 00:07:23,392
The most advanced ones are made

128
00:07:23,622 --> 00:07:26,712
with machines that only one company
in the world builds.

129
00:07:27,432 --> 00:07:28,632
It is Dutch.

130
00:07:28,831 --> 00:07:32,111
When Washington wanted to slow down
China's chip industry,

131
00:07:32,471 --> 00:07:35,131
the Netherlands and Japan
joined export restrictions

132
00:07:35,361 --> 00:07:37,451
on advanced chipmaking equipment.

133
00:07:38,121 --> 00:07:39,741
Or take money itself.

134
00:07:40,521 --> 00:07:44,521
Most of the world's foreign currency
reserves are still held in US dollars.

135
00:07:45,221 --> 00:07:49,161
That helps the United States borrow
more cheaply than it otherwise could.

136
00:07:49,784 --> 00:07:54,064
A French finance minister
had a name for that in the 1960s.

137
00:07:54,644 --> 00:07:56,204
An exorbitant privilege.

138
00:07:56,986 --> 00:07:58,546
Bases. Hospitals.

139
00:07:58,806 --> 00:07:59,866
Export controls.

140
00:08:00,096 --> 00:08:04,246
Sanctions that actually bite,
when key partners join them.

141
00:08:04,326 --> 00:08:08,146
And an alliance that went to war
when the US was attacked.

142
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:10,340
None of that is free.

143
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:13,520
But almost none of it
is bought with money either.

144
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:15,460
It is bought with trust.

145
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:19,580
So when the trust goes,
the allies are left with options.

146
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:22,660
And a country with options can say no.

147
00:08:23,255 --> 00:08:25,615
No to the next round of export controls.

148
00:08:26,355 --> 00:08:28,295
No to the next sanctions package.

149
00:08:28,835 --> 00:08:31,455
No to the base. No to the airspace.

150
00:08:32,015 --> 00:08:34,475
No to the vote at the United Nations.

151
00:08:35,241 --> 00:08:36,561
Not out of hostility.

152
00:08:36,721 --> 00:08:40,401
But because saying no
no longer costs what it used to.

153
00:08:43,031 --> 00:08:45,771
And here is what makes it so hard to undo.

154
00:08:46,412 --> 00:08:49,912
Suppose the next government
in Washington changes course.

155
00:08:50,692 --> 00:08:54,112
It apologises,
renews every promise, and means it.

156
00:08:54,595 --> 00:08:56,875
The factory in Europe is already built.

157
00:08:57,435 --> 00:08:59,355
The new supplier has been tested.

158
00:09:00,115 --> 00:09:01,815
The contracts are signed.

159
00:09:02,375 --> 00:09:05,075
None of that disappears
when relations improve.

160
00:09:05,655 --> 00:09:09,075
And the allies have learned something
that cannot be unlearned.

161
00:09:09,235 --> 00:09:13,255
A promise from Washington
may last only until the next election.

162
00:09:13,745 --> 00:09:17,065
So even the best promise
now comes with an expiry date.

163
00:09:17,685 --> 00:09:19,305
That is the real cost.

164
00:09:19,805 --> 00:09:22,885
Not one bad deal,
but every future deal worth less.

165
00:09:25,645 --> 00:09:28,545
None of this means
the United States is collapsing.

166
00:09:29,465 --> 00:09:32,365
It still has the strongest military,
the deepest markets,

167
00:09:32,495 --> 00:09:35,065
and the world's most important currency.

168
00:09:35,712 --> 00:09:39,072
But power was never only
what a country can do on its own.

169
00:09:39,732 --> 00:09:42,252
It was also what others did
because it asked.

170
00:09:42,957 --> 00:09:45,677
For eighty years,
when something went wrong in the world,

171
00:09:45,757 --> 00:09:47,717
the first call went to Washington.

172
00:09:48,357 --> 00:09:50,717
Not because anyone had to call.

173
00:09:50,797 --> 00:09:53,497
Because everyone assumed it would answer.

174
00:09:54,707 --> 00:09:56,647
The allies have not become enemies.

175
00:09:57,087 --> 00:10:01,347
They are still trading, still talking,
still sitting at the same tables.

176
00:10:02,037 --> 00:10:05,337
They have just started
keeping other numbers on speed dial.

177
00:10:05,995 --> 00:10:10,815
And in Denmark, forty-four families still
remember the cost of answering that call.

178
00:10:11,824 --> 00:10:13,664
The call came from Washington.

179
00:10:16,084 --> 00:10:18,564
If you are watching this
in the United States,

180
00:10:18,644 --> 00:10:21,144
this is not just a story
about your allies.

181
00:10:21,904 --> 00:10:24,584
It is about what your country
stands to lose.

182
00:10:25,291 --> 00:10:28,211
Some of these changes
are already difficult to reverse.

183
00:10:29,111 --> 00:10:30,951
Others are still being decided.

184
00:10:31,683 --> 00:10:36,883
The United States is losing the advantages
of trust, one quiet decision at a time.

185
00:10:37,463 --> 00:10:38,793
And that is still a choice.
